theorem fibonacci-natural
  note        By induction on k: F(0) and G(0) are 0 and 1, and the next
              pair is G(k) and F(k) + G(k), sums of natural numbers.
  let F(0) = 0,  G(0) = 1,
      F(k + 1) = G(k),  G(k + 1) = F(k) + G(k),  for k ∈ ℕ₀           (D1)
         reads the Fibonacci numbers, F(k) the kth and G(k) the next
  then for all k ∈ ℕ₀, F(k) ∈ ℕ₀ and G(k) ∈ ℕ₀

1.  For all k ∈ ℕ₀, F(k) ∈ ℕ₀ and G(k) ∈ ℕ₀.
    induction on k starting at 0

    base
    1.1.  F(0) = 0
          D1

    1.2.  G(0) = 1
          D1

    1.3.  F(0) ∈ ℕ₀
          membership, from 1.1

    1.4.  G(0) ∈ ℕ₀
          membership, from 1.2

    1.5.  F(0) ∈ ℕ₀ and G(0) ∈ ℕ₀
          join 1.3, 1.4

    step
    let k ∈ ℕ₀                                                        (K)
    assume step 1 is true for k, the induction hypothesis             (IH)

    1.6.  F(k + 1) = G(k)
          D1

    1.7.  G(k + 1) = F(k) + G(k)
          D1

    1.8.  F(k + 1) ∈ ℕ₀
          membership, from IH, 1.6

    1.9.  G(k + 1) ∈ ℕ₀
          membership, from IH, 1.7

    1.10. F(k + 1) ∈ ℕ₀ and G(k + 1) ∈ ℕ₀
          join 1.8, 1.9

theorem fibonacci-recurrence
  note        Each Fibonacci number is the sum of the two before it: F(k + 2)
              is G(k + 1), which is F(k) + G(k), and G(k) is F(k + 1).
  let F(0) = 0,  G(0) = 1,
      F(k + 1) = G(k),  G(k + 1) = F(k) + G(k),  for k ∈ ℕ₀           (D1)
         reads the Fibonacci numbers, F(k) the kth and G(k) the next
  then for all k ∈ ℕ₀, F(k + 2) = F(k) + F(k + 1)

1.  For all k ∈ ℕ₀, F(k + 2) = F(k) + F(k + 1).
    proof
    let k ∈ ℕ₀                                                        (K1)

    1.1.  F((k + 1) + 1) = G(k + 1)
          D1

    1.2.  G(k + 1) = F(k) + G(k)
          D1

    1.3.  F(k + 1) = G(k)
          D1

    1.4.  F((k + 1) + 1) = F(k) + G(k)
          transitivity, from 1.1, 1.2

    1.5.  F((k + 1) + 1) = F(k) + F(k + 1)
          substitute F(k + 1) = G(k) (line 1.3) into line 1.4

    1.6.  F(k + 2) = F(k) + F(k + 1)
          substitute
            F((k + 1) + 1) = F(k) + F(k + 1)      1.5
            F(k + 2) = F(k) + F(k + 1)            algebra
